<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #eaf2f8;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Can I Have a Pencil? – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Polite Request</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Can I Have a Pencil?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Respond to basic questions about a familiar story. Interactive: Summarize spoken stories.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can respond to basic questions about a class story using appropriate phrases and expressions (e.g., "What is his name?" "I'm sorry, I don't know."). Can summarize a spoken dialogue about asking for help or making a polite request in two sentences (e.g., "He needs a pencil." "He asks for a pencil."). Can recognize familiar phrases from a dialogue by raising hands when they hear key phrases (e.g., "Please," "Can I?," "I'm sorry.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows real classroom objects (a pencil, paper, a bag, a desk, a table) and uses flashcards. The teacher points to each item and says its name clearly, "This is a pencil." "This is paper." Students listen and repeat chorally. The teacher asks, "What do you have in your bag?" to activate prior knowledge about school supplies.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the phrase "Can I have a pencil, please?" by reaching for a pencil and using gestures. The teacher emphasizes the polite tone. The teacher then models "I can't roll the dice" by pretending to try and fail, showing inability. The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /p/ in "please" and /k/ in "can't," asking students to listen and repeat the sounds and words. The teacher also blends sounds for "kind" (/k-aɪ-n-d/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(After modeling "Can I have a pencil, please?") "Am I asking for something or giving something?" (Expected answer: "Asking for.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(After modeling "I can't roll the dice.") "Can I roll the dice?" (Expected answer: "No, you can't.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Polite Request."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher uses a short, illustrated audio dialogue (or teacher-read with puppets/gestures) featuring two students, Ana and Tom, at their desks. The dialogue introduces polite requests and responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Ana:</b> "Oh no! I don't have a pencil for my paper."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Tom:</b> "Do you need a pencil, Ana?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Ana:</b> "Yes, please, Tom. Can I borrow your pencil?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Tom:</b> "Sure! Here you go. I have an extra pencil."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Ana:</b> "Thank you, Tom! You are very kind."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Tom:</b> "My pleasure!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will listen for the main idea and specific details in a simple dialogue to understand a polite request for an object and the polite response. Pragmatic: Making polite requests: "Can I have a pencil, please?" Offering assistance politely: "Do you need help?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher plays/reads the dialogue twice.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "Who needs a pencil?" (Ana) "Does Tom help Ana?" (Yes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Students show thumbs up for True, thumbs down for False):</b> "Ana has a pencil." (False) "Tom gives Ana a pencil." (True) "Ana says 'thank you'." (True)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific target grammar (Polite requests: "Can I have...please?", Negative contracted form of can: "I can't...") and vocabulary (pencil, friend, ask, need, borrow, give, please, thank you, kind, my pleasure). Phonemic Awareness: Blending of spoken phonemes to form "kind" (/k-aɪ-n-d/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Listening Gap-fill:</b> The teacher distributes a simple handout with the dialogue from Stage 2, but with key words missing (e.g., "Can I have a ____, please?", "Thank ___!"). The teacher plays/reads the dialogue again, and students listen and fill in the blanks. They check answers with a partner quietly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Identify Phrases:</b> The teacher reads the dialogue again, sentence by sentence, slightly slower. Students are instructed to raise their hand when they hear specific target phrases or words: "please," "thank you," "Can I," "pencil," "friend," "my pleasure," "kind." The teacher pauses to reinforce the pronunciation of /p/ in "please" and /k/ in "can't" if applicable, and /k-aɪ-n-d/ in "kind."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Listening for Inability:</b> The teacher presents a few simple scenarios orally. Students listen for the word "can't" and show a "no" gesture with their hands. For example: "I can't reach the book." (Teacher shows inability). "My friend can't answer the question." "We can't go outside now." Sociolinguistic: Expressing inability politely.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "A Polite Request."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Listen and Sequence the Story.</b> The teacher tells a new, slightly varied, short story about needing a classroom item, incorporating the target vocabulary and grammar. Students are given a set of 3-4 simple picture cards (pre-drawn or drawn by students quickly) that represent key events from the story. Students listen carefully and arrange the cards in the correct sequence on their desks.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Story:</b> "Maria is at her desk, ready for art class. Oh no! She needs paper for her drawing, but she doesn't have any. She looks at her friend, Leo. Maria asks politely, 'Leo, can I have some paper, please?' Leo smiles and says, 'Yes, of course, Maria! Here's some paper.' Leo gives Maria the paper. Maria says, 'Thank you, Leo! You are a helpful friend!' Leo answers, 'My pleasure!'"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Students' ability to listen for the sequence of events, identify who needs what, and understand the polite exchange. Fluency of understanding and demonstrating comprehension of the request and response is key.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Picture Cards (examples):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Card 1: Maria looking sad at her empty desk.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Card 2: Maria asking Leo for paper (speech bubble with "?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Card 3: Leo giving Maria paper.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Card 4: Maria drawing happily with the paper.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe students' ability to correctly sequence the story pictures and respond to basic questions during the activity. The teacher circulates and listens as students work in pairs or small groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will respond to basic "Wh-" questions about the story they just sequenced from Stage 4. The teacher asks students individually or in small groups.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who needs paper?" (Maria)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does Maria ask for?" (Paper)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does Leo help Maria?" (Yes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does Maria say after she gets the paper?" (Thank you)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Can correctly sequence 3 out of 4 story pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Retrieval:</b> Can answer at least 3 out of 4 basic questions about the story correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Recognition of Phrases:</b> Can identify target polite phrases (e.g., "please," "thank you") when heard.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick self-reflection by circling "Yes" or "No" on a small paper slip or raising hands.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I learned to listen for polite words like 'please' and 'thank you'." (Yes / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can understand who needs a pencil or paper in a story." (Yes / No)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about school things did you hear today?" (e.g., pencil, paper, desk, friend)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to understand the stories about asking for things politely?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson? (e.g., The use of realia and illustrated dialogues helped comprehension. Students successfully identified key polite phrases.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Some students found "I can't" challenging to distinguish from "I can." Need more explicit practice for this in future lessons. Perhaps slower pacing for the sequencing activity for some students.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson introduced core vocabulary and phrases for polite requests through listening. In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will engage with a short illustrated text that uses these same vocabulary words and polite request structures to further deepen their understanding of the context and meaning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>